your favorite foods giant.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.627
Today, I have my friend Alvin visiting me

00:00:21.627 --> 00:00:24.270
and I think he has a
special challenge for me.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.460
Kind of nervous, but a little bit excited.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.777
So, Alvin, come on out.

00:00:27.777 --> 00:00:29.172
- Yo, yo.

00:00:29.172 --> 00:00:31.050
- Hi, welcome.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.310
- Thank you for having me.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:33.570
I have something special for you.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:34.560
- I'm kind of nervous.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:35.460
- Look, I'm a big dude,

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:36.450
this is "Making it Big."

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:37.530
I'm gonna do it nice and slow.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.350
- Oh.
- McDonald's apple pie.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.060
- I'm already loving this challenge.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.890
I have a few questions for you though.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.120
How big exactly?

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.310
- I'm talking big. Big baby big.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:48.270
- Big baby big?

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.830
- Yeah. I think you can make it happen.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.350
- Does it have to look exactly like this?

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.330
- I would think that you
have to nail the box.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.550
The box is the key to my nostalgia.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.385
- And then how about these
little like design on it?

00:00:59.385 --> 00:01:00.218
- The lattice?

00:01:00.218 --> 00:01:01.410
- Yeah, the lattice.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:02.520
- The lattice is pretty huge.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.353
- Okay, fine.

00:01:03.353 --> 00:01:04.290
- You know what it is too?

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.820
Is we need to know that it's an apple pie.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.410
- I guess it's time for me

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:11.551
to make my version of
the McDonald's apple pie.

00:01:11.551 --> 00:01:12.384
- Boom.

00:01:12.384 --> 00:01:15.960
- So first things first, I
need to start on the pie dough,

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.080
and this is five pounds of
flour right into the bowl.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.410
Then I'm going to add some sugar and salt.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.770
Taking my little whisk,
whisking this all together.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:29.220
This is my favorite part of
making pie dough, is the butter.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.250
I have two pounds of butter in here

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:36.510
so that means two pounds
of delicious-ness.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.760
And then I'm going to coat the butter,

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.160
all of this flour together.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.440
And then now I'm taking
this pastry blender

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.380
aka my Wolverine tool

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.670
to really mash in the butter
and the flour together.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:54.540
I just need it to get into
the consistency of wet sand.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.360
So when I think about fast food apple pie,

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.900
I always think that it's a
luxury because growing up,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.840
just ordering desserts
in general was a luxury.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.680
As soon as I was able
to make my own money,

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.980
it was apple pie all day, every day.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:13.410
I am very happy with my dough.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:16.410
I'm going to add in
some ice into this water

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.480
because I want it to be ice cold.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.900
And this right here is
the secret ingredient:

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.310
apple cider vinegar.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.450
This is what's going to make
my pie crust really flaky.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:32.220
I don't like flaky people,
but I love flaky pie, so...

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.880
And then I'm going to add
three cups of this water

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:40.880
into my pie dough, but
no ice goes in the water.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:45.630
Oh my god. There's ice, there's ice.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.430
Oh, that's much better.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.290
Look at me using my big brain.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:52.530
Gonna take my spatula and just fold it in.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.980
And we are making a total of two batches

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.110
because we need one dough for the bottom

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.820
and then one dough to cover the top.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:03.540
Now I'm gonna go in with my
hand and just kind of mold it.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.540
Making pie dough is so tricky
because it seems very easy

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:10.140
but it's not.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.550
Baking in general seems
like, "Yeah," but it's not.

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.910
This is taking everything
in me to not knead it,

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:19.440
but you can't knead pie dough

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.810
because it'll start to develop gluten

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:24.390
and that won't give you a flaky crust,

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:25.800
and we want a flaky crust.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.890
I'm very happy with
how this came together.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.810
Gonna lay down some saran wrap.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.299
And now I bring this bad boy over.

00:03:33.299 --> 00:03:34.626
Oh my god.

00:03:34.626 --> 00:03:35.459
Oh.

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:39.270
I'm going to shape this
into a big rectangle

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:42.540
so it's easier for me
to roll it out later.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.790
I'm really happy with this shape.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.100
And I'm gonna just wrap this up.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.140
All right, so this is my first one.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.490
I'm going to make one more

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.523
and then let them both rest for an hour.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.100
So to make this filling,

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.220
we're going to use this giant apple.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.053
Just kidding.

00:04:00.053 --> 00:04:04.650
We're using just regular sized
apples, just 50 pounds of it.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.840
In order to make a giant apple pie,

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.650
I have to peel and chop a lot of apples.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.200
I have a lot of work, so
I'm gonna go right into it.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.380
This is only therapeutic
for like two, three apples

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.960
and then everything just
kind of turns into a blur.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.010
So the first time I had
a fast food apple pie

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:26.280
was when I was in
culinary school actually.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.720
It was actually perfection.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.690
And now I have this
handy dandy apple cutter

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.070
and apple corer.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:35.010
I'm just gonna go ahead and go for it.

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:38.193
This is my first time using this.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.950
Oh, I like this.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.780
Let's keep peeling.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.300
I'm using both green and red apples

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.730
because the green apple
holds up really well

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:50.340
when it's cooking but
it's a little bit tart,

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.063
so I'm mixing it with the
red so it's a nice balance.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:59.820
I don't know if you have to be
that violent, but I gotta be.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:02.040
It looks like I still
have a lot of work to do,

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.579
so I'll check back in about like an hour.

00:05:05.579 --> 00:05:10.579
And 50 pounds of peeled and
cut apples later, I'm here.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.270
And now I'm going to pour some lemon juice

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:18.510
right over the top of
this to prevent browning

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.943
and to also cut the
sweetness from the apples.

00:05:20.943 --> 00:05:25.380
Then I'm gonna go over here
adding some cornstarch to a bowl

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.270
along with some sugar.

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:31.890
This is nine cups of sugar,
some salt and some cinnamon.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:35.490
And this will be my cinnamon sugar mixture

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:37.770
that will go right into the apples.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.320
And so I'm gonna mix this together

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.810
with my hands so I could
feel that the apples

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:46.020
are really gripping
onto the cinnamon sugar.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:50.430
This is a bit overwhelming
'cause this is a lot of apples.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.080
Wait, I kind of like this.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.060
Instead of like playing with slime,

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.920
I feel like this should be the new wave.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:56.930
Ah, there you...

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.130
There's a lot of liquid down
here at the bottom of the bowl

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:03.450
and I'm really happy about that

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:04.980
because when I cook it down

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:08.820
that's going to give it that
like syrupy is all going to...

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:10.420
Look at my nails poking through.

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:15.060
Okay, so these are ready.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.643
I'm gonna take them to
the stove and cook them.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.540
Well this is the biggest
pan that I could find

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.480
and I have all five burners on.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.033
I'm just gonna add in
two sticks of butter.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.120
Yum. So the butter's melted.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.100
Now I have to add the apples

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.750
but I'm probably gonna
have to phone a friend

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:36.753
because I can't carry that and
put it in at the same time.

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:41.490
Oh, well this is only half of the batch

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:43.410
'cause I don't wanna overcrowd the pan.

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.470
I kind of wanna take a bath in this.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:50.730
So apparently, McDonald's
apple pie was the first dessert

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:54.240
to be added to their menu in 1968.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:56.310
That's older than my mom.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:00.960
So the original apple pie
was fried instead of baked

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:02.130
like how it is today.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.600
I would prefer it fried.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.100
Can they bring that back?

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.320
McDonald's uses a variety

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:10.800
of six different American grown apples:

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:14.430
Fuji, Gala, Golden Delicious, Ida Red,

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:17.490
Jonagold and Rome apples.

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:20.490
The filling is done and it looks so good.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.230
It smells even better by the way.

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:25.650
I'm gonna need some help
carrying this to over there.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:26.483
Kelly.

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:29.340
Here we go.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.320
I'm hungry.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.550
This looks so good.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:33.720
This is only one batch.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:35.790
I have another batch to cook down.

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:39.330
After that I'm gonna cool them
and put them in the fridge.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:42.240
Now it's time to roll out my dough.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.630
I have like roughly 20
pounds of dough right here

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.940
both for the bottom and for the top.

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.370
Gonna roll the bottom out to the size

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:52.230
of this sheet pan right here.

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:55.590
So I'm dusting my surface
and my rolling pin with flour

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:57.883
just so my dough doesn't stick.

00:07:57.883 --> 00:08:00.510
Oh, let's get one out.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:02.370
I feel like I'm unwrapping a present.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.760
It is a present actually
that I made for myself.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.790
Regular pies doughs already
difficult to roll out as is,

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:13.265
like, just regular size,
but this is a whole block.

00:08:13.265 --> 00:08:14.300
(exhales sharply) Let's go.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:19.300
(fast dramatic music)
(Tway groaning)

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:21.690
I feel like this is going nowhere.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:26.690
This pie is definitely going
to hit after this workout.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:29.280
Alvin better like this pie or else.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.640
So this part is actually
not only difficult

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:35.550
because it's 10 pounds of pie dough,

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:38.310
but I have to make sure that it stays cold

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:39.930
so the butter doesn't melt.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:41.310
I'm gonna flip it over again

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:43.290
just so the butter doesn't get warm.

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:45.390
And about 10 minutes later,

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:50.390
I am here with a full sheet
pan-size of pie dough.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:55.290
I think I am ready to
transport this into the tray.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:57.780
And I do have a special little thing

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.700
I wanna introduce to you guys.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:03.300
This is called a pan extender

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:06.630
and you basically put
it in the baking tray

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:10.950
and this is going to
support the pie filling.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.020
I'm gonna roll this dough up.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.210
My heart is beating so fast

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:19.260
and okay, we're in.

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:23.370
Now I'm just kind of just
pinching the dough to the edge

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:24.630
just so it can stay up.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:28.110
So the hardest part about
making pie is the temperature.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:30.030
You have to keep it at a cold temperature

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:31.470
so the butter can stay put.

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:32.760
If the butter starts to melt,

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:36.390
then the whole pie dough
starts to kind of fall apart,

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:38.940
and then in the oven it
falls apart, it gets soggy,

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:40.830
and then you get a soggy pie.

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:42.090
And we don't want a soggy pie.

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:44.370
We want a nice and flaky pie.

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:46.860
This is all nice and folded.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:50.130
Now I have to put all of
this filling into the pie

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:52.950
and I gotta move fast.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:54.450
Oh yeah.

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.430
This is so much apple.

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:57.480
If you've ever wanted to see

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:01.470
what 50 pounds of pie filling
looks like, this right here.

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:04.080
Then I'm gonna fold this in.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.320
And the egg wash is basically an adhesive

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:10.020
for the top dough to stick.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:12.540
Okay, I'm nervous.

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:15.120
This is so technical.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:17.760
I'm gonna trim off some extra pie dough

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.700
on the side right here so
it's easier for me to tuck it.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:22.320
And the reason why I'm tucking it

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.910
is because we are making sure
that it's nice and sealed

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:28.980
so the filling doesn't spill out.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:32.940
Whoever said it's as easy
as pie, they're lying.

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:34.770
They've never made high before.

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:36.240
This is pretty warm,

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.310
so I'm gonna pop this in the freezer

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:41.250
so I can cut out the little design on top.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:44.730
So I made a special little stencil

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:48.690
that I'm going to use to
cut the top of the pie.

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:50.850
I'm gonna lay my stencil down here.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:53.610
I have my paring knife and my ruler.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:54.780
I'm ready. Let's go.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:57.780
I'm feeling kind of nervous,

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:00.870
but the ruler's giving
me a lot of confidence.

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:02.457
Oh, it moved. Okay.

00:11:02.457 --> 00:11:03.750
And I gotta move fast

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:06.060
because this is nice and cold right now

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:08.430
and I don't want it to melt.

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.050
Oh, that looks so good.

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:12.480
This stencil was such a good idea

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:15.851
because this would've been
a mess with no stencil.

00:11:15.851 --> 00:11:18.810
(sighs) Moment of truth.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:19.643
Yay.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:24.870
Egg wash, so it can look nice and golden.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:28.950
And then I'm going to sprinkle
on some turbinado sugar.

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:31.440
This is to add some texture to the pie,

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:34.470
and also visually, it looks
really, really pretty.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:36.570
Into the oven this goes.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.493
I hope that it works out.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:41.883
I need someone to help me please.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:46.174
Wow.

00:11:46.174 --> 00:11:47.310
Okay.

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:50.280
So this is actually 70 pounds

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:53.940
because it's 50 pounds
of filling by itself

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:56.640
and then we have to account for the dough,

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:58.620
and that's another 20 pounds,

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:01.860
so 70 pounds of pie.

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:06.860
And this big baby is going to
stay in here for four hours.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:08.070
Good luck.

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:09.540
I'll see you in four hours.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.580
All right, so the pie is done.

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:13.350
The box is also done.

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:15.600
I'm really, really excited
for Alvin to see this,

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:18.862
so Alvin, please come on out.

00:12:18.862 --> 00:12:21.945
- (laughs) Yo, what?

00:12:23.705 --> 00:12:24.913
(Alvin laughing)

00:12:24.913 --> 00:12:26.640
- I can't believe this is happening.

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:27.577
- [Tway] What's your rating on the box?

00:12:27.577 --> 00:12:28.800
